00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.710 align:start position:0%
rivers run deep with history  epic tales of the landscapes and  societies that neighbor their shores  not to mention their own winding  heritage  these old channels are paths the  mississippi river has taken in the past  at one time its waters even flowed into  the atchafalaya river  for decades the mississippi river has  tried to carve its way back to the  achafalaya river  practically bursting at the seams  through what's called an avulsion  a river of vulces or breaks its banks  when sediment chokes up its channel  and reduces its carrying capacity enough  that the water is forced to find a more  efficient path  the u.s army corps of engineers has kept  the river in check  using a combination of floodgates and  levees called the old river control  system  human engineering might have brought  this river to heel but it requires  constant vigilance and not all rivers  are so compliant  in 2008 levees on the kosi river  ruptured the flood swept across india  and took more than 400 lives  while displacing another three million  in the aftermath of the flood the indian  government built even more levees  river scientists now say that higher  levees may actually do more harm than  good  that's because they constrain a river  and force it to dump  sediment within its channel raising its  riverbed and reducing the amount of  water it can safely carry  using models and laboratory tools called  stream tables  researchers are getting better at  understanding when and where  an avulsion might occur there are two  key hot spots  the first is at the foot of the  mountains where rivers slow  down models show that in this region  the slowing rivers tend to drop  sediments and silt up their channels  leading to an avulsion but what about  river deltas  where slope isn't a factor here  the interaction between river flow and  ocean tides determines where the river  is likely to evolve  flooding in the lower parts of the delta  washes away sediment  setting the table for avulsions upstream  at the head of the delta  scientists modeling the mississippi  river have found that its natural  avulsion point would be right near the  old river control  while promising these models can account  for human effects  like deforestation upstream which adds  silt to the river  or climate change which is raising sea  levels  and structures like levees and gates  will only age and  fail with time making long-term  solutions critical  along china's yellow river engineers are  pursuing soil conservation projects  and ways to flush out sediment deposits  better predictions could lead to better  solutions perhaps grounded in lessons  we've learned  from the complex history that links  rivers and people  [Music]  you
 

